# Cursor AI Rules - Example

This is an example of what will be generated when users run the CLI.

## Instructions

Read and follow the standards defined in the `.ai/` directory.

## Priority Order

1. **Custom rules first**: Check `.ai/custom/` for project-specific customizations
2. **Standards**: Then consult `.ai/standards/` for coding standards and architecture patterns
3. **Test patterns**: Use `.ai/test-patterns/` when generating tests
4. **Heuristics**: Apply `.ai/heuristics/` to guide testing decisions

## When Generating Code

- Follow naming conventions from `.ai/standards/coding-standards.md`
- Apply architectural patterns from `.ai/standards/architecture-patterns.md`
- Use best practices from `.ai/standards/best-practices.md`
- Maintain consistency with existing codebase

## When Generating Tests

- Follow the appropriate test pattern from `.ai/test-patterns/`
  - `unit.md` for unit tests
  - `integration.md` for integration tests
  - `e2e.md` for end-to-end tests
  - `api.md` for API tests
  - `performance.md` for performance tests
- Apply QA heuristics from `.ai/heuristics/qa-heuristics.md`
- Use AAA pattern (Arrange-Act-Assert)
- Include happy path, edge cases, and error cases
- Aim for meaningful test coverage

## Code Quality

- Write clean, maintainable code
- Use TypeScript for type safety
- Handle errors appropriately
- Write self-documenting code
- Add comments only when necessary to explain "why", not "what"

## Security

- Validate all inputs
- Never expose secrets in code
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Sanitize user data
- Follow OWASP guidelines

## Performance

- Optimize for readability first, performance second
- Use memoization when appropriate
- Lazy load heavy components
- Implement proper caching strategies

---

**Remember**: These rules are guidelines. Always consider the specific context and requirements of the task at hand.

